.TH ARCH 9
.SH NAME
arch \- the architecture interface: traps, descriptor tables, paging, the panic screen, per-CPU state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:arch"
.PP
.nf
.B "early_init :: proc ()"
.B "init_traps :: proc (cpu_id: u64)"
.B "set_trap_handler :: proc (h: Trap_Handler)"
.B "set_interrupt_handler :: proc (vector: u8, h: Interrupt_Handler)"
.B "set_user_trap_handler :: proc (h: User_Trap_Handler)"
.B "set_syscall_dispatcher :: proc (h: proc ""c"" (frame: ^Trap_Frame))"
.B "syscall_init :: proc ()"
.B "timer_available :: proc () -> bool"
.B "timer_attach :: proc (virt: uintptr)"
.B "irq_route :: proc (line: int, vector: u8) -> bool"
.B "load_kernel_space :: proc (root: uintptr)"
.B "load_address_space :: proc (root: uintptr)"
.B "current_address_space :: proc () -> uintptr"
.B "flush_page :: proc (virt: uintptr)"
.B "leaf_encode :: proc (phys: uintptr, flags: Page_Flags) -> Page_Table_Entry"
.B "entry_address :: proc (e: Page_Table_Entry) -> uintptr"
.B "frame_ip :: proc (frame: ^Trap_Frame) -> uintptr"
.B "frame_sp :: proc (frame: ^Trap_Frame) -> uintptr"
.B "thread_user_init :: proc (frame: ^Trap_Frame, entry, sp: uintptr, arg0: u64)"
.B "frame_enter_user :: proc (frame: ^Trap_Frame, entry, sp: uintptr, arg0: u64)"
.B "percpu_critical_depth :: proc () -> ^int"
.B "percpu_id :: proc () -> int"
.B "in_interrupt :: proc () -> bool"
.B "yield_now :: proc ()"
.B "ipi_send :: proc (target: u32, vector: u8)"
.B "cpu_class :: proc () -> Cpu_Class"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Arch
is the one package the rest of the kernel may import for anything that
touches the CPU directly.
It is a name list, not code: each architecture supplies one
.BI arch_ arch .odin
file, selected by build tag, that binds every name below to its own
implementation.
Adding a port means one new binding file and no changes to a call site in
.B kernel/sched
or
.BR kernel/mem .
.B kernel/arch/neutral
holds the vocabulary two or more ports have to agree on byte for byte:
.BR Trap_Kind ,
.BR Page_Flag ,
.BR Serial_Kind ,
.BR Cpu_Class ,
the paging constants, and the table-index arithmetic.
It imports nothing, so every port imports it, and a mismatched copy of one
of these enums cannot exist.
Vectra amd64's binding is
.BR kernel/arch/arch_amd64.odin ,
which imports
.B kernel:arch/amd64
and
.BR kernel:arch/neutral .
arm64 and riscv64 bind the same names against their own packages; see
.IR ports (9).
.PP
Each port provides:
.IP \(bu
.B early_init
turns on whatever the first Odin statement after it needs.
On amd64 that is SSE: Odin's codegen uses XMM registers for a plain
struct assignment, and Limine base revision 5 and later clears
.B CR4.OSFXSR
before the kernel runs.
.IP \(bu
.B init_traps
installs the descriptor tables: the GDT and TSS, then the IDT.
Safe to call before
.B kernel:mem
exists, because the fault stacks are static.
.IP \(bu
paging encode and decode:
.BR leaf_encode ,
.BR branch_encode ,
.BR entry_address ,
.BR entry_flags ,
.B table_index
and
.BR level_size .
.B kernel/mem/vmm.odin
owns the page table walk and calls through these only to encode what it
decided.
.IP \(bu
a timer:
.BR timer_available ,
.BR timer_physical_base ,
.BR timer_attach ,
.B timer_calibrate
and
.BR timer_ack .
amd64's is the local APIC, calibrated against the PIT.
.IP \(bu
an interrupt controller:
.BR irq_available ,
.BR irq_route ,
.B irq_set_mask
and
.BR irq_ack .
amd64's is the I/O APIC.
.IP \(bu
a per-CPU record, reached through
.B percpu_id
and
.BR percpu_critical_depth ,
which
.B sync.can_sleep
reads to count spinlocks held on the current core; see
.IR sync (9).
.IP \(bu
the system call stub, armed by
.B syscall_init
and pointed at the portable dispatcher by
.BR set_syscall_dispatcher .
.IP \(bu
context init and resume:
.BR thread_resume_init ,
.B thread_user_init
and
.BR frame_enter_user .
The first two lay out a
.B Trap_Frame
and an FXSAVE image on a thread's own kernel stack, in the place the trap
tail would have left them, so a new thread and a resumed one are entered
by the same
.B iretq
at the end of the same tail.
.B frame_enter_user
does the same with the selectors set for ring 3.
.SS The trap frame
.B Trap_Frame
holds the general-purpose registers, pushed by the entry stub, and the five
words the CPU itself pushed on a privilege change: the vector and error
code, then
.BR rip ,
.BR cs ,
.BR rflags ,
.B rsp
and
.BR ss .
Field order is the stub's push order and does not move for tidiness.
.B rsp
and
.B ss
are the interrupted stack, not the handler's, which is what makes a fault
on a bad stack reportable at all.
A
.B Resume
pairs a
.B ^Trap_Frame
with its FXSAVE image; a thread switch is a switch of which
.B Resume
the trap tail resumes.
.B frame_ip
and
.B frame_sp
read the frame without naming a register, so
.B kernel:user
never sees one directly.
.SS The panic screen
A trap that reaches
.B kernel/panic.odin
with no handler claiming it stops the machine.
The report renders into the same console chassis the boot splash draws, so
a panicking machine still looks like the one that booted, then goes to the
kernel log on both the screen and the serial port.
Three rules bound the code that builds it.
Nothing allocates, because the heap may be what broke.
Nothing can fault twice: a re-entry guard is checked before the framebuffer
is touched, and a second fault on this core halts directly rather than
looping.
Nothing returns except the one recoverable case, the boot self-test's own
breakpoint.
The report gives the trap's name and vector, the ring it came from, the
error code, the faulting address for a page fault, the instruction and
stack pointers, every register, and a backtrace built by scanning the
kernel stack for words that land inside the kernel's own text, since
Vectra keeps no frame pointer to chain.
Every slot the scanner reads is validated against the page tables first.
.SS The user trap handler
.B arch.set_user_trap_handler
installs a second table, separate from the kernel's own trap handler.
A fault in the kernel is a bug in this image and reaches the panic screen.
A fault in a program is an ordinary outcome: the installed handler records
the fault in the process's own exit record, wakes whatever is waiting on
it, and ends the thread through
.BR sched.kill_current .
It runs in interrupt context, on the faulting thread's kernel stack, with
interrupts already off, so it allocates nothing, takes no lock and logs
nothing directly.
A page fault a program can recover from, a write to a copy-on-write page
or a page of its own not yet mapped, is fixed in place and the faulting
instruction runs again; see
.IR space (9).
